      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-252933",
      ":question/problem": "Let $X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}, X_{4}$ be four independent random variables, each chosen from $N(1, 3^{2})$ What is the approximate probability that the maximum of these variables is less than or equal to $5$?\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1.2$ | $0.8849$ | $0.8869$ | $0.8888$ | $0.8907$ | $0.8925$ |\n| $1.3$ | $0.9032$ | $0.9049$ | $0.9066$ | $0.9082$ | $0.9099$ |\n| $1.4$ | $0.9192$ | $0.9207$ | $0.9222$ | $0.9236$ | $0.9251$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
    }
